<head>
    <title>Reactivity | Nijor</title>
    <meta name="keywords" content="Reactivity in Nijor, Nijor Reactivity, Reactive State, Bindings, Refs"/>
    <meta name="description" content="Learn about Nijor's unique reactivity model. Master reactive variables, derived state, DOM refs, and two-way data bindings.">
</head>

<body layout="docs">

    <section>
        <h1>Reactivity in Nijor</h1>
        <p>
            Reactivity is the framework's capability to automatically update parts of the user interface when underlying application state changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nijor features a highly performant and intuitive reactivity model. Reactive variables are declared and managed using a special prefix, completely bypassing the need for standard JavaScript variable declarations like <span class="txt">let</span> or <span class="txt">const</span>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>Declaring Reactive State</h1>
        <p>
            You declare a reactive variable using the prefix <span class="txt">$.</span> inside your <span class="txt">&lt;script&gt;</span> block. 
            Once declared, you can render this state in your template using curly brace interpolation <span class="txt">\{$.variableName}</span>.
        </p>
    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <h1>Counter : \{$.count}</h1>
        </template>

        <nscript>
            $.count = 0;
        </nscript>
    </code>
        <p>
            Whenever the value of <span class="txt">$.count</span> changes, Nijor pinpoints the text node and updates the DOM directly.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>Updating Reactive State</h1>
        <p>
            To update reactive state, assign a new value to the reactive variable. 
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Important Event Signature Rule:</strong> Any event handler function in your <span class="txt">&lt;script&gt;</span> that modifies reactive state must accept the reactivity context <span class="txt">$</span> as its last parameter. When calling the event handler from the template, you do not pass the <span class="txt">$</span> argument explicitly; Nijor's compiler injects it automatically at runtime.
        </p>
    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <h1>Counter : \{$.count}</h1>
            <button on:click="increment()">Increase</button>
            <button on:click="decrement()">Decrease</button>
        </template>

        <nscript>
            $.count = 0;

            export function increment($) \{
                $.count++;
            }

            export function decrement($) \{
                $.count--;
            }
        </nscript>
    </code>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>Derived Reactive State (Computed Properties)</h1>
        <p>
            Derived reactive variables are values that depend entirely on other reactive state. Nijor automatically tracks these dependencies and recalculates the derived variable whenever its parent state changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            You declare derived state using a JavaScript label with a colon prefix <span class="txt">_ :</span> followed by the assignment:
        </p>
    <code lang="js" shiki>
        $.count = 0;
        _ : $.double = $.count * 2;
    </code>
        <p>
            In the example below, <span class="txt">$.double</span> depends on <span class="txt">$.count</span>. Whenever <span class="txt">$.count</span> increases or decreases, <span class="txt">$.double</span> is recalculated and any bound DOM nodes automatically refresh.
        </p>
    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <h2>Original Count : \{$.count}</h2>
            <h2>Doubled Value : \{$.double}</h2>
            <button on:click="increment()">Add 1</button>
        </template>

        <nscript>
            $.count = 0;
            _ : $.double = $.count * 2;

            export function increment($) \{
                $.count++;
            }
        </nscript>
    </code>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>DOM References <span class="txt">n:ref</span></h1>
        <p>
            If you need to access raw DOM nodes directly (e.g., to focus an input, trigger video playback, or read a form element's current property), use the <span class="txt">n:ref</span> attribute.
        </p>
        <p>
            When applied, Nijor registers the DOM element directly onto your reactivity context as <span class="txt">$.varName</span>:
        </p>

    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <input type="text" nref="{$.username}" placeholder="Enter name" />
            <button on:click="showValue()">Show Value</button>
        </template>

        <nscript>
            export function showValue($) \{
                console.log($.username.value); // Access the native DOM node
            }
        </nscript>
    </code>

        <p>This works seamlessly with other elements, such as dropdown selects:</p>

    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <select nref="{$.theme}">
                <option value="light">Light Mode</option>
                <option value="dark">Dark Mode</option>
            </select>

            <button on:click="printTheme()">Print Theme</button>
        </template>

        <nscript>
            export function printTheme($) \{
                console.log($.theme.value);
            }
        </nscript>
    </code>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>One-Way Data Binding <span class="txt">n:bind</span></h1>
        <p>
            To bind a user input's value directly to a reactive state variable in real time, use the <span class="txt">n:bind</span> attribute. 
            This sets up a one-way sync between your DOM element and your reactive state :
        </p>
        <p>
            It is fully supported on <span class="txt">input</span> tags, <span class="txt">textarea</span> elements, and any container containing the <span class="txt">contenteditable="true"</span> property.
        </p>

    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <h1>নমস্কাৰ \{$.name || "Stranger"}!</h1>
            <input nbind="{$.name}" placeholder="Type your name..." />
        </template>
    </code>

        <p>
            As you type inside the input, <span class="txt">$.name</span> is updated dynamically, causing the welcome header to immediately reflect the typed text.
        </p>

        <h3>Binding to Textareas & Contenteditable Elements</h3>
    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <textarea nbind="\{$.message}"></textarea>
            <p>Characters : \{$.message.length}</p>
        </template>
    </code>

    <code lang="html" shiki>
        <template>
            <div contenteditable="true" nbind="\{$.text}"></div>
            <p>Raw text : \{$.text}</p>
        </template>
    </code>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h1>Key Rules & Guidelines</h1>
        <p>
            The global prefix <span class="txt">$</span> is reserved exclusively for Nijor's reactivity system. Never re-declare or override the <span class="txt">$</span> variable manually inside your components.
        </p>
        <p>
            Always ensure your state-modifying functions receive the <span class="txt">$</span> argument in their function signature.
        </p>
        <p>
            Reactivity variables do not require initialization using <span class="txt">let</span>, <span class="txt">var</span>, or <span class="txt">const</span>; simply write <span class="txt">$.myVar = value</span> directly inside your component scripts.
        </p>
    </section>

</body>